  @doc ~S"""
  Routing provides the capability to modify network requests that are made by a page.

  Once routing is enabled, every request matching the url pattern will stall unless it's continued, fulfilled or aborted.

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > The handler will only be called for the first url if the response is a redirect.

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > `PlaywrightEx.Page.route/4` will not intercept requests intercepted by Service Worker. See [this](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/issues/1090) issue. We recommend disabling Service Workers when using request interception by setting `:service_workers` to `:block`.

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > `PlaywrightEx.Page.route/4` will not intercept the first request of a popup page. Use `PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.route/4` instead.

  **Usage**

  An example of a naive handler that aborts all image requests:

  ```elixir
  page = Browser.new_page!(browser)
  Page.route!(page, "**/*.{png,jpg,jpeg}", &Route.abort/1)
  Page.goto!(page, "https://example.com")
  Browser.close!(browser)
  ```

  or the same snippet using a regex pattern instead:

  ```elixir
  page = Browser.new_page!(browser)
  Page.route!(page, ~r/(\.png$)|(\.jpg$)/, &Route.abort/1)
  Page.goto!(page, "https://example.com")
  Browser.close!(browser)
  ```

  It is possible to examine the request to decide the route action. For example, mocking all requests that contain some post data, and leaving all other requests as is:

  ```elixir
  Page.route!(page, "/api/**", fn route ->
    if Route.request(route) |> Request.post_data() |> String.contains?("my-string") do
      Route.fulfill!(route, body: "mocked-data")
    else
      Route.continue!(route)
    end
  end)
  ```

  If a request matches multiple registered routes, the most recently registered route takes precedence.

  Page routes take precedence over browser context routes (set up with `PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.route/4`) when request
  matches both handlers.

  To remove a route with its handler you can use `PlaywrightEx.Page.unroute/3`.

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > Enabling routing disables http cache.

  *Since Playwright 1.8.*

  ## Arguments

  * `url` - A glob pattern, regex pattern, URL pattern, or predicate that receives a `URL` to match during routing. If `:base_url` is set in the context options and the provided URL is a string that does not start with `*`, it is resolved using the [`new URL()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URL/URL) constructor.
  * `handler` - handler function to route the request.

  ## Options

  * `:times` (`t:integer/0`) - *Since Playwright 1.15.* How often a route should be used. By default it will be used every time.
  """
  @spec route(
          t(),
          String.t() | Regex.t() | PlaywrightEx.RegExp.t() | (String.t() -> boolean()),
          (PlaywrightEx.Route.t() -> term()) | (PlaywrightEx.Route.t(), PlaywrightEx.Request.t() -> term()),
          route_options()
        ) :: {:ok, PlaywrightEx.Disposable.t()} | {:error, PlaywrightEx.error()}
  def route(page, url, handler, options \\ []) do
    options = Facade.options!(options, @route_options, "page.route")
    Facade.async("page.route", page, fn -> PlaywrightEx.Client.Page.route(page, url, handler, options) end)
  end

  @doc "Like `route/4`, but returns the value and raises on error."
  @spec route!(
          t(),
          String.t() | Regex.t() | PlaywrightEx.RegExp.t() | (String.t() -> boolean()),
